2. Significant Accomplishments

2.1 Itcl/Java Exploration

In the previous monthly report, we discussed the possible advantages
that the Java language offers for use in our context, particularly as a
replacement for C++. Based on that analysis, we are exploring using a
combination of the Itcl scripting language and Java. Both are portable,
network-savvy, and object-oriented. Java is about 10 times faster that
Itcl, so for computationally intensive applications, calling Java
methods makes a lot of sense. For high-level control and dynamic
reconfiguration, Itcl offers a higher-level interface.

Sun's Tcl/Java interface, available at
ftp://ftp.sunlabs.com/pub/tcl/tcljava0.4.tar.gz, can be used to create
a shared library that is loaded by the Java byte interpreter. This
shared library has Java classes that allow the user to evaluate Tcl
commands. Since Itcl is an extension of Tcl, this provides a platform
for building an interface between classes in Itcl and classes in Java.
Christopher Hylands created a GNU autoconf file that allows
tcljava to be easily configured and installed from within Tycho.

Christopher Hylands also extended the tycho script extended so that if
it is passed a -java command line argument, then the java program is
called, the tcljava interface is started, the Itcl, Itk and Iwidgets
libraries are loaded and Tycho is started. The main difference between
calling tycho -java and just tycho is that with tycho -java the java
program is the executable that is running, whereas with tycho (with no
-java), the itkwish program is the executable that is running.

2.1.1 Java/Tcl performance

Sun's Tcl/Java interface allows us to call Tcl commands from within
Java and to call Java functions from within Tcl. In a simple test
using the factorial function, we found that using Java to compute the
factorial of 1 was about half the speed of using Tcl. However, Java
was about 1.76 times faster than Tcl in computing the factorial of
2. The performance of Java increases as we compute the factorial of
higher numbers. This indicates that there is overhead in calling just
one Java method, but Java is faster if recursion is involved.

