2.1.2 TclJava limitations

  1. We need a way to compile and load Java classes on the fly from within
     Tycho.
  2. Tcljava has does not fully implement the Tcl exec or after commands.
     This will probably change over time.
  3. Tcljava currently works only under Unix. This too will probably change
     with later releases of the tcljava interface.
  4. The Sieve demo is hardwired together, rather than being built from
     functional blocks by the user. However, this demo does prove that it is
     possible to use Java threads from within Tycho. The Ptolemy group is
     meeting regularly to discuss further implementation of the PN domain
     within Tycho and Java.


2.1.3 Process Network Domain in Java and Tycho

Tom Park's Process Network (PN) domain uses threads via C++. Tom has
written a Sieve of Eratosthenes demo in Java that uses the Java threads
to compute the first N prime numbers. Extending Tycho to generate Java
code for the PN domain would allow PN domain demos to run on many
different platforms.

In the ideal world, we would have a PN domain code generated that was
itself written in Java. The generated code would run standalone outside
of Tycho using Java classes from a small library.

Once we have a PN domain working, we could start on DDF and SDF
(dynamic dataflow and synchronous dataflow). One end result could be a
Java application building tool.

Since we can call Java functions from within Tcl, it is fairly easy to
write a Java class that calls Tom Park's Sieve of Eratosthenes Process
Network (PN) Java code and then return a Tcl list containing the first N
primes.


2.1.4 Using the Java appletviewer within Tycho

The Java appletviewer may be faster than the Tk canvas for certain
operations. We would like to be able to use the appletviewer as a Tk
widget to display Java graphical code. However, rewriting the
appletviewer in either Tk or C code would be a fairly large task.
Instead, we would like to reparent the appletviewer X window inside our
Tk application.

The TkSteal Tcl/Tk extension, written by Sven Delmas allows us to
reparent X windows such as the appletviewer. TkSteal is available at
http://www.cimetrix.com/sven/tksteal.html.

Christopher Hylands used TkSteal to reparent the appletviewer inside a
wish. There are a number of rough edges to be worked out here, but the
technique shows some promise.

2.1.5 TkSteal/appletviewer limitations

TkSteal and the appletviewer have the following limitations.

  1. The appletviewer flashes up on the screen before getting remapped into
     the Itcl wish application. TkSteal suggests a few ways around this:
        o Use the -iconic option to start up the application iconified.
          Unfortunately, appletviewer does not have this option.
        o Use the -geometry option to start the application up off the
          screen. Unfortunately, appletviewer does not have this option
          either.
     It might be possible to add these options by deriving a class from the
     sun.applet.AppletViewer Java class.
  2. The appletviewer does not take a -name option so we can't hardwire in
     the window name and then pass that name to TkSteal. However,
     appletviewer does use the filename it was passed to create the window
     name. For example, if appletviewer Blink.html is called, then the
     window will be named Applet Viewer: Blink. We pass this name to
     TkSteal.
  3. We have not been able to reparent appletviewer inside Tycho. This is
     probably a minor namespace issue.
  4. Creating an Itcl class to handle the details of using the appletviewer
     would help greatly.


