2.3 Mutable Systems

Ron Galicia has worked out a proposal to modify static
hardware/software partitioning techniques (some of which come from our
group) to support mutable systems, or systems whose configuration and
functionality changes dynamically during operation.

The extension he proposes would generate an architecture for a family
of specifications instead of a single specification.  It would address
manufacturing issues ("What target should I choose to minimize
retooling between these different applications and/or versions?") and
adaptable or mutable computing ("What minimum target can support these
different applications and on-the-fly switching between them?").  The
central research question is: What is the impact of a family of
specifications (FOS) on existing codesign methodologies?  More
specific issues include:

1) What should the input to an FOS codesign flow be?
   a) How should the specifications/FOS be represented?
   b) How should the libraries be represented?

2) How much better is an FOS methodology at addressing manufacturing
   flexibility/reuse and mutability than existing design flows?

3) What changes must be made to existing codesign algorithms to
   accommodate FOS?

4) What tools are needed to design with FOS in mind?

We expect that 1a will leverage off of the current and future work in
control and dataflow semantics and that 1b will differ from typical
library representations because it will include multiprocessor
components (such as those in the Mercury RACEway). Item 2 would require
the entry of some representative demos and the creation of the
necessary component libraries to determine quality of the
implementations. Item 3 would be along the lines incorporating FOS into
existing heuristic objectives. Item 4 would leverage use Tycho.


